1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,600
You hand over a piece of paper.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,900
You get something real back. Why does that work?

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,400
Start with the paper itself. It's cotton and linen, ink, and a number.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:21,400
A hundred-dollar bill costs the U.S. government about nine cents to make.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,900
Whatever you're being handed, it isn't nine cents of paper.

6
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:29,400
Before money, a trade needed a double coincidence of wants.

7
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,400
I have fish and want shoes — you must want fish, right now.

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:39,900
Money is the fix: a middle step everyone accepts, so trades stop needing luck.

9
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,400
For most of history that middle step was a thing: shells, salt, cattle, gold.

10
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:48,400
Gold can't feed you or heat your house.

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:52,900
It worked because it was scarce, hard to fake — and because everyone agreed.

12
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:57,400
Until 1971, dollars were still formally convertible into gold.

13
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,400
That August, President Nixon suspended it. The link was cut.

14
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:07,900
What's left is fiat money — Latin for 'let it be done'. It's worth something because we say so.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,400
And most money was never printed at all.

16
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:16,900
The Bank of England puts it plainly: about 97% of money is bank deposits.

17
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:22,900
Numbers in a database. When a bank makes a loan, it types new money into existence.

18
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:27,400
So money is not real the way a rock is real.

19
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:34,900
It's real the way language, laws and borders are real: agreements with enormous consequences.

20
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:39,400
What holds it up isn't magic. Taxes are owed in it.

21
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:43,400
Law makes it settle debts. Its supply is limited and managed.

22
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,400
And underneath all of it — confidence.

23
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,400
Take the confidence away and you get Germany in 1923, or Zimbabwe in 2008.

24
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:54,900
The paper didn't change. The belief did.

25
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:59,600
Money isn't a thing we found. It's an agreement we renew every single day.
